// Test: a function declared at the top level is callable from anywhere at the
// top level, including above its own declaration — and that the hoisting stops
// at the top level.
//
// Nobody had decided this; the architecture decided it, differently per engine.
// The register VM compiles the file before running it and so registered every
// name first, while the tree-walker and the browser engine bound each name as
// its statement executed. `>> f(2) ¶` written above `f(x) { <~ x * 10 }` printed
// 20 under `--vm` and was `undefined function: 'f'` in the default engine (DM-03)
// — and `zymbol check` passed it in both, because zymbol-semantic had already
// taken the hoisting side. The analyzer promised what one engine in three kept.

// 1. Called before it is declared.
>> diez(2) ¶

diez(x) {
    <~ x * 10
}

// 2. Still callable after, which is the ordinary case.
>> diez(3) ¶

// 3. Mutual recursion between two functions declared in the order that needs
//    hoisting: `par` calls `impar`, which is declared below it.
>> par(4) ¶
>> par(7) ¶

par(n) {
    ? n == 0 { <~ #1 }
    <~ impar(n - 1)
}

impar(n) {
    ? n == 0 { <~ #0 }
    <~ par(n - 1)
}

// 4. The limit, upwards: inside a block, a top-level function declared *below*
//    the block is visible, because that one is hoisted.
? #1 {
    >> tardia(5) ¶
}

tardia(x) {
    <~ x - 5
}

// Hoisting is top level only, matching what the VM's compiler already did. A
// function declared *inside* a block is not covered here on purpose: the
// semantic analyzer never collected those declarations, so calling one is
// `undefined function` even from inside its own block — while the program runs
// anyway and exits 0. That is DM-23, and it predates this file.
